---
interface Props { top?: boolean }
// On its own page the case title is the h1 and each beat label an h2; inside
// the one-page version they sit one level lower. Beat labels are headings so
// screen-reader users can jump beat to beat.
const { top = false } = Astro.props;
const HT = top ? 'h1' : 'h2';
const HL = top ? 'h2' : 'h3';
const HD = top ? 'h3' : 'h4'; // decision titles
import Split from './Split.astro';
import CaseContents from './CaseContents.astro';
import DecisionMap from './dg/DecisionMap.astro';
import Personas from './dg/Personas.astro';
import Journey from './dg/Journey.astro';

import Pic from './Pic.astro';
import ProductCapture from './ProductCapture.astro';
import Demo from './Demo.astro';
import Flow from './dg/Flow.astro';
import Stack from './dg/Stack.astro';
---
<section id="ai" data-tone="dark" class="band">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="chapter-head">
      <div class="g" style="row-gap:28px;align-items:end">
        <div style="grid-column:1/9;display:grid;gap:24px">
          <p class="eyebrow">Private alpha · practice, not a shipped product</p>
          <HT class="h-chapter" style="view-transition-name:t-ai">uoaui.ai</HT>
        </div>
        <div class="avatar-practice" style="grid-column:1/13"><Pic name="shannon-building.png" alt="My pixel-art avatar holding a laptop" sizes="320px" widths={[320, 640]} /><p class="lead muted">A design platform I'm building on Claude. It turns a prompt into a working screen built from real design systems, Salt, Material, Fluent and Carbon among them, with their official React components. Switch system, density or theme and the screen rebuilds.</p></div>
        <div class="meta" style="grid-column:1/13"><span><b>Role</b> · Designed and built it, solo</span><span><b>Status</b> · Private alpha</span><span><b>Live</b> · <a href="https://uoaui.ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">uoaui.ai<span class="vh"> (opens in new tab)</span> →</a></span></div>
      </div>
      <CaseContents project="ai" />
    </header>

    <div class="beat rv" id="ai-what">
      <div class="label"><HL class="k"><b>01</b> The problem underneath</HL></div>
      <div class="body">
        <Split as="h3" class="h-sub" text="Designing for output you can't fully predict." />
        <ProductCapture name="live-uoaui.png" alt="The live uoaui.ai comparison page showing the same finance screen in Salt and uoaui" href="https://uoaui.ai/" label="uoaui.ai · Private alpha" />
        <ProductCapture name="live-uoaui-builder.png" alt="The current authenticated uoaui builder showing the Salt Performance Analytics canvas, chat and component library" href="https://uoaui.ai/builder" label="uoaui.ai · Current builder · Private alpha" />
        <ul class="rows">
          <li><b>Designing for output you can't predict</b><p>Where the system acts and where it asks. How a person stays in control without making the AI useless. The compare and tune steps are my answer so far. The model drafts, I decide.</p></li>
          <li><b>Who it's for, and the trap</b><p>Designers like me, which is dangerous, because designing for yourself hides your blind spots. The alpha exists partly to break my own assumptions.</p></li>
          <li><b>What it isn't</b><p>It doesn't ship the final product and I don't claim it does. It closes the gap between a Figma file and the thing a developer can inspect.</p></li>
        </ul>
        <Personas project="ai" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="beat rv" id="ai-flow">
      <div class="label"><HL class="k"><b>02</b> The flow</HL></div>
      <div class="body">
        <Split as="h3" class="h-sub" text="Prompt to production screen." />
        <Flow
          label="The builder flow, prompt to export"
          steps={[
            { t: 'Prompt', d: 'Describe the screen in words' },
            { t: 'Generate', d: "Built from a system's official React components" },
            { t: 'Compare systems', d: 'The same screen in Salt, Material, Fluent and Carbon' },
            { t: 'Tune', d: 'Density, theme and charts. The person decides here' },
            { t: 'Export React', d: 'Code a developer recognises' },
          ]}
          caption="The model drafts. Compare and tune are where I decide, before anything exports."
        />
        <Demo src="/media/uoaui-current.mp4" poster="/media/uoaui-current-poster.webp" width={1280} height={800} label="The current uoaui walkthrough: choose a template, edit a card and present the screen across five systems." />
        <ProductCapture name="live-uoaui-charts.png" alt="The current uoaui UI Kit charts and data visualisation specimens in Salt" href="https://uoaui.ai/ui-kit" label="uoaui.ai · UI Kit · Charts and data visualisation" />
        <Journey project="ai" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="beat rv" id="ai-decisions">
      <div class="label"><HL class="k"><b>03</b> Decisions and trade-offs</HL></div>
      <div class="body">
        <DecisionMap project="ai" />
        <ol class="dec">
          <li><span class="n">01</span><div><HD>Official components only, never overridden</HD><p>We explored letting the model draw free-form UI and restricting it to each system's published React components. Free-form looked impressive and was unusable.</p><p class="verdict">Official-only won. The output is something a developer recognises, at the cost of the model having fewer moves.</p></div></li>
          <li><span class="n">02</span><div><HD>Five systems, one model underneath</HD><p>I mapped their tokens, density scales and theming into one structure so a screen can switch system without being rebuilt by hand.</p><p class="verdict">Where the systems disagree, I chose the strictest definition each time.</p></div></li>
          <li><span class="n">03</span><div><HD>Compare and tune before export</HD><p>The model's first answer is a draft. I compare across systems and tune the screen before I export.</p><p class="verdict">That's where the person decides, and where I correct the model most often.</p></div></li>
        </ol>
        <Stack
          label="How a screen is built, from the person to the exported code"
          layers={[
            { t: 'The person', d: 'Writes the prompt, then compares and tunes before anything exports.', side: 'Where decisions happen' },
            { t: 'The model', d: 'Claude drafts the screen. A draft, never the final answer.' },
            { t: 'Token mapping', d: 'Tokens, density scales and theming for five systems in one structure. Where they disagree, the strictest definition wins.', mine: true, side: 'The part I designed' },
            { t: 'Official components', d: "Each system's published React components, never overridden.", side: 'Salt · Material · Fluent · Carbon · my own' },
            { t: 'Export', d: 'React a developer can inspect.' },
          ]}
          legend="The layer I designed"
          caption="Switch system, density or theme and the screen rebuilds, because every layer below the model is mapped, not generated."
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="beat rv" id="ai-judgement">
      <div class="label"><HL class="k"><b>04</b> AI at work</HL></div>
      <div class="body">
        <Split as="h3" class="h-sub" text="Where Figma still matters, and where a designer still does." />
        <ul class="rows">
          <li><b>Figma for foundations</b><p>The better the design file, the better the AI output. At Barclays I'm matching Figma naming to the code so there's no gap in either direction. When the model can't get the spacing right, I point it at the file. Everything after that, I'd rather iterate in code.</p></li>
          <li><b>Built from the terminal</b><p>Both public rebuilds in this portfolio were made with Claude Code and Codex, in days, to test decisions with real-shaped data. Winking Star shipped the same way.</p></li>
          <li><b>Where a designer still matters</b><p>A colleague brought me a screen made with AI. It looked fine, used none of our components, and nobody had tested it with a user. I rebuilt it in our system, showed both, and we could ship in two weeks instead of months. A generated screen still needs someone who watched how people work.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
